#!/bin/sh
#
# script/metrics: the project's own numbers, one row per ISO week, and the charts a reader meets
# them in (notes/project-metrics.md).
#
# Why this exists. calef asked for it on 2026-09-02: milestone counts by status over time, decision
# counts by status over time, lines of code and comments over time, coverage over time, and then
# a `BUGS` count, "visible in github and linked from the README". The complaint it answers is
# notes/register-of-measures.md's own opening, one file over: this tree measures a great deal and
# remembers almost none of it. That register says which numbers this kernel owes itself; this
# script is the half that says which ones moved, and when.
#
# **Every row is a RESTATEMENT, not a report.** The series is regenerated from git history by
# applying TODAY's definitions to old commits. That is the right choice for a trend and it is not
# what was reported at the time, and this project has three same-day proofs that the difference is
# real: on 2026-09-02 alone, milestone 214 found 80 sites that printed a skip line and returned
# while counting as passes, milestone 212 found this tree's falsification ratio walking `crates/`
# only, and DECISIONS 139 corrected a figure that conflated a counter's tick resolution with the
# cost of reading it. A dashboard makes numbers look authoritative. notes/project-metrics.md says
# on its face that these are restatements, and this comment is the same warning where the numbers
# are actually produced.
#
# **The naming columns are the sharpest instance of that hazard in this file** (milestone 276). A
# name's provenance lives in the header of the thing it names, and that convention was invented by
# milestone 115 on 2026-08-04. Three weeks of the series predate it, so they restate as a tree full
# of named things and no recorded ratifications, which is a convention's birth rather than a fact
# about the names. `names_total` counts the named things and the four status columns count the ones
# whose block parses, so those weeks show the gap instead of hiding it. notes/project-metrics.md
# says the same thing where the bars are.
#
# What it does NOT build, and why:
#
#   - **Coverage is not re-derived here, and that is a rule about this script rather than about
#     the measurement.** It needs an instrumented build, and nothing here is allowed to build or
#     check anything out (see `--backfill` below), so the column is only ever filled from an lcov
#     file a caller already has. The weekly workflow fills the current week that way. A past week
#     can be filled the same way with `--coverage-for`: check out that week's commit somewhere
#     else, run THAT tree's own `script/coverage` on THAT tree's pinned nightly, and hand this
#     script the lcov it wrote. That is what the live cells measured, so the number means the same
#     thing. notes/project-metrics.md records which weeks were backfilled that way on 2026-09-19,
#     on which toolchains, and the two that could not be measured exactly.
#   - **No plotting dependency.** DECISIONS §46 (thin primitives or whole subsystems, and we
#     write everything in between) makes taking one a decision rather than a shortcut, and a
#     stacked bar is string formatting. The SVGs below are written by hand and a diff can review them.
#
# Usage:
#
#   script/metrics                 print the current commit's row as a table
#   script/metrics --csv           print the current commit's row as one CSV line
#   script/metrics --backfill      rewrite every week's row from git history, then the charts
#   script/metrics --update        recompute THIS week's row in place, then the charts
#   script/metrics --check         non-zero if --update would change anything, a missing week
#                                  included. NOTHING RUNS IT: it cannot gate a pull request,
#                                  because every merge moves the current week's numbers.
#   script/metrics --charts        regenerate the SVGs from the CSV without touching the CSV
#
#   --coverage-from <lcov.info>    fill the CURRENT week's coverage cell from an lcov file the
#                                  caller produced (`script/coverage` writes
#                                  target/llvm-cov/lcov.info). With --update or --backfill it
#                                  lands on the week HEAD is in and on no other week.
#   script/metrics --coverage-for <WEEK> --coverage-from <lcov.info>
#                                  write that one week's coverage cell and redraw the charts.
#                                  Refuses a week the CSV does not already have, and changes no
#                                  other cell: the row is not recomputed, so its commit and every
#                                  count stay exactly as they were. The lcov must come from that
#                                  week's own commit; nothing here can check that, so say which
#                                  commit and toolchain in the commit message. (Flag name
#                                  provisional, 2026-09-19.)
#
# Name: provisional. Minted 2026-09-02. `metrics` is a noun, which DECISIONS §134 puts on the
# reporting half of the `script/` family (`names`, `citations`, `decisions`, `roadmap`,
# `coverage`, `falsifications` report; `verify`, `test`, `fuzz`, `bench` do something), and this
# only reports. Considered and set aside: `dashboard`, which names the rendering rather than the
# numbers and would go stale the day the charts changed shape; `measures`, which collides with
# notes/register-of-measures.md, a different thing (what the kernel owes itself, rather than what
# moved this week). The directory `notes/project-metrics/` holding the CSV and the SVGs follows
# this name and moves with it. calef has not ratified it.
#
# **One spelling of a week, everywhere.** A week is `2026W36`: the chart axis, the CSV's `week`
# column, this script's own output and the prose on `notes/project-metrics.md` all use it. calef
# ratified the form on 2026-09-02 after seeing three spellings in one page (`2026-W30` in the
# narrative, `202636` on an axis, `2026-W29` in the data), which is two more than a reader should
# have to hold.
#
# It is ISO 8601's designation with the hyphen dropped. It still sorts lexically, it still cannot be
# mistaken for an ordinary number the way a bare `202636` can, and it is the dictionary key that
# makes a rerun replace a row rather than append one.
#
# BUGS
#
# - **Which files carry a name is written twice**: here in `name_surfaces`, and in `script/names`'
#   SURFACES table. The parse is shared (`scripts/name_provenance.py`); the enumeration is not,
#   because this script reads a historical `git ls-tree` and that one reads the working tree. The
#   two drifted once already: milestone 175 moved programs to `components/` and `fixtures/` on
#   2026-09-13, `script/names` was told and this was not, and `names_total` showed a collapse from
#   204 to 133 that never happened until 2026-09-19. Nothing compares the two counts. The cheap
#   check a reader can run is that the current week's `names_total` equals `script/names`' total.
# - **The coverage cell is carried, not recomputed.** A `--backfill` or `--update` keeps whatever
#   coverage a week already had, because it cannot be re-derived without a build. So after the row
#   is repointed at a later commit, the coverage figure describes an earlier commit of that week
#   than every other cell in the row, until someone runs `--coverage-for` against the new commit.
#   2026W38 was in that state from 2026-09-19 until the backfill the same day re-measured it.
# - **Nothing ties an lcov file to a commit.** `--coverage-for` believes the caller about which
#   week the file measured. An lcov produced from the wrong checkout is written without complaint.
# - **Charts are checked by eye, in a browser.** QuickLook crops an SVG's right edge and made the
#   legends look clipped on 2026-09-19 when they were not; render with a browser before believing a
#   layout defect.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

exec python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
import math
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from datetime import datetime, timezone

# The derivations this script shares with `script/lint`, in one place rather than copied into both
# (milestone 236: three derivations are copied between scripts, and nothing notices when they
# drift). The import works because this script `cd`s to the repository root before it runs python,
# which is what made a shared module possible without turning a `#!/bin/sh` entry point into the
# consumer of a built artifact. What stays here rather than moving is the file SOURCING below: this
# script reads blobs at historical revisions and never checks anything out, and that tolerance is
# deliberately not in the shared count.
# `dont_write_bytecode` because the import would otherwise leave a `scripts/__pycache__/`
# behind on every run, which is an untracked directory a gate has no business creating.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, 'scripts')
import name_provenance                                                      # noqa: E402
import roadmap_proposals                                                    # noqa: E402
import rust_source                                                          # noqa: E402
from rust_source import non_blank, strip_non_code                           # noqa: E402

PAGE = 'notes/project-metrics.md'
DATA_DIR = pathlib.Path('notes/project-metrics')
CSV_PATH = DATA_DIR / 'weekly.csv'

MILESTONE_STATUSES = ['BUILT', 'PARTIAL', 'IN-PROGRESS', 'REMOVED',
                      'NOT-STARTED', 'OPTIONAL', 'RECORDED']
DECISION_STATUSES = ['DECIDED', 'AMENDED', 'SUPERSEDED', 'PROPOSED', 'UNRECORDED']
# The naming vocabulary is `script/names`', taken from the shared module rather than retyped, so a
# fifth word added there arrives here as a column instead of as a silent undercount. It is lower
# case where the other two are upper, because these are the words a `Name:` block literally spells.
NAME_STATUSES = name_provenance.STATUSES


def _col(prefix, status):
    return prefix + '_' + status.lower().replace('-', '_')


FIELDS = (['week', 'commit', 'date']
          + [_col('milestones', s) for s in MILESTONE_STATUSES] + ['milestones_total']
          + [_col('decisions', s) for s in DECISION_STATUSES] + ['decisions_total']
          + [_col('names', s) for s in NAME_STATUSES] + ['names_total']
          + ['proposals_unnumbered']
          + ['kernel_code_lines', 'kernel_comment_lines',
             'other_code_lines', 'other_comment_lines',
             'bugs_markdown', 'bugs_rust',
             'harnesses_falsified', 'harnesses_unfalsified', 'harnesses_total',
             'unsafe_outside_arch', 'unsafe_inside_arch', 'unsafe_thread_safety',
             'unsafe_code_lines', 'unsafe_density',
             'fatal_risks_tested', 'fatal_risks_untested',
             'coverage_lines_pct'])


def git(*args):
    return subprocess.run(['git', *args], capture_output=True, check=True).stdout


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading a commit's tree without touching the working tree.
#
# `git show <rev>:<path>` per file would be several thousand processes over nine weeks. One
# `git ls-tree -r -z` names the blobs and one `git cat-file --batch` streams every one of them, so
# a whole week's tree costs two processes. Nothing is ever checked out: AGENTS.md's rule against
# `git checkout <path>` exists because it destroyed uncommitted work three times, and a backfill
# that walks history is exactly where that mistake would be made.

# What a week's tree has to carry. `.rs` and `.md` are the line, comment, `BUGS`, harness and
# `unsafe` counts. `Cargo.toml` and `script/` joined them for the naming columns (milestone 276):
# a package's provenance block lives in its manifest, because a package here may have a `lib.rs`, a
# `main.rs` or neither, and a `script/` entry point's lives in its shell comment header. Widening
# the filter costs about sixty more blobs in the same single `cat-file --batch`, and changes nothing
# else, because every other metric selects by suffix out of the same dictionary.
def _wanted(path):
    if path.startswith(('vendor/', 'target/')):
        return False
    return (path.endswith(('.rs', '.md', '/Cargo.toml'))
            or (path.startswith('script/') and path.count('/') == 1))


class Tree:
    """Every blob a metric reads in one commit: Rust, markdown, manifests and `script/` entries."""

    def __init__(self, rev):
        self.rev = rev
        paths, oids = [], []
        for entry in git('ls-tree', '-r', '-z', rev).decode('utf-8', 'replace').split('\0'):
            if not entry:
                continue
            meta, path = entry.split('\t', 1)
            _mode, kind, oid = meta.split()
            if kind != 'blob' or not _wanted(path):
                continue
            paths.append(path)
            oids.append(oid)
        self.text = {}
        if not oids:
            return
        blob = subprocess.run(['git', 'cat-file', '--batch'],
                              input=('\n'.join(oids) + '\n').encode(),
                              capture_output=True, check=True).stdout
        pos = 0
        for path in paths:
            end = blob.index(b'\n', pos)
            size = int(blob[pos:end].split()[2])
            self.text[path] = blob[end + 1:end + 1 + size].decode('utf-8', 'replace')
            pos = end + 1 + size + 1

    def rust(self):
        return [(p, t) for p, t in self.text.items() if p.endswith('.rs')]

    def markdown(self):
        return [(p, t) for p, t in self.text.items() if p.endswith('.md')]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The metrics. Each one takes a Tree and returns numbers; none of them builds anything.

# The roadmap index row: `| 231 | NOT-STARTED | [title](file) | note | date |`. The status
# vocabulary is checked rather than assumed, because the same file holds a vocabulary table whose
# rows also start with a pipe, and because `design/roadmap.md` (this directory's predecessor,
# split by milestone 76) used the same shape with a different set of tokens.
_INDEX_ROW = re.compile(r'^\|\s*(\d+)\s*\|\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9 _-]*?)\s*\|')


def milestones(tree):
    counts = dict.fromkeys(MILESTONE_STATUSES, 0)
    text = tree.text.get('design/roadmap/README.md') or tree.text.get('design/roadmap.md')
    if not text:
        return counts
    seen = set()
    for line in text.split('\n'):
        match = _INDEX_ROW.match(line)
        if not match:
            continue
        number, status = match.group(1), match.group(2)
        if status in counts and number not in seen:
            seen.add(number)
            counts[status] += 1
    return counts


def decisions(tree):
    """Decision counts by status, and the honest bucket for the years before there was one.

    `design/decisions/README.md` is the index milestone 114 split `DECISIONS.md` into, and it is
    the first version of that record to carry a status per decision at all. Before it, a decision
    was a `## N.` heading in one 5,320-line file and nothing said whether it still held. Those
    weeks are counted as UNRECORDED rather than as DECIDED, because reading them as decided would
    invent a status the record never carried, which is the failure `script/names` refuses one
    directory over.
    """
    counts = dict.fromkeys(DECISION_STATUSES, 0)
    text = tree.text.get('design/decisions/README.md')
    if text:
        seen = set()
        for line in text.split('\n'):
            match = _INDEX_ROW.match(line)
            if not match:
                continue
            number, status = match.group(1), match.group(2)
            key = 'SUPERSEDED' if status.startswith('SUPERSEDED') else status
            if key in counts and number not in seen:
                seen.add(number)
                counts[key] += 1
        if sum(counts.values()):
            return counts
    legacy = tree.text.get('DECISIONS.md')
    if legacy:
        counts['UNRECORDED'] = len(set(re.findall(r'^## (\d+)\.', legacy, re.M)))
    return counts


# The four kinds of named thing `script/names` knows about, enumerated out of a tree's paths
# rather than off the disk. This is the half milestone 236's rule says stays at the caller: the
# parse of a `Name:` block is shared (`scripts/name_provenance.py`), and the file sourcing is not,
# because `script/names` is asked about the tree in front of it and this is asked about eight
# revisions nobody has checked out.
#
# A package is every `Cargo.toml` outside `crates/` and outside the workspace root, discovered
# rather than listed, which is the property `script/names` built the kind for: a package that
# arrives tomorrow is counted without editing anything. Its name is the package's, not the
# directory's, because that is the name a `use` statement and a dependency list carry.
_PACKAGE_NAME = re.compile(r'^\s*name\s*=\s*"([^"]+)"', re.M)


def name_surfaces(tree):
    """(name, path of the file that must carry its block, comment prefix), one per named thing."""
    crates, out = set(), []
    for path in tree.text:
        parts = path.split('/')
        if any(part.startswith('.') or part == 'target' for part in parts[:-1]):
            continue
        if parts[0] == 'crates' and len(parts) > 2:
            crates.add(parts[1])
        # Programs lived in `user/src/` until milestone 175 split it into `components/src/` and
        # `fixtures/src/` on 2026-09-13. This enumeration was not told, so from 2026W37 about ninety
        # programs dropped out of `names_total` (204 to 133) and the chart showed a collapse that
        # never happened, while `script/names`, which was told, went on reading 222. All three
        # directories are read because history holds both layouts and a week is counted from the
        # tree it had. The enumeration is still a second copy of `script/names`' SURFACES table;
        # the BUGS entry in this file's header says so rather than pretend it cannot drift again.
        elif (len(parts) == 3 and parts[1] == 'src' and path.endswith('.rs')
              and parts[0] in ('user', 'components', 'fixtures')):
            out.append((parts[2][:-3], path, '//!'))
        elif len(parts) == 2 and parts[0] == 'script':
            out.append((parts[1], path, '#'))
        elif parts[-1] == 'Cargo.toml' and parts[0] != 'crates':
            hit = _PACKAGE_NAME.search(tree.text[path])
            if hit:
                out.append((hit.group(1), path, '#'))
    for crate in crates:
        out.append((crate, 'crates/%s/src/lib.rs' % crate, '//!'))
    return out


def names(tree):
    """Naming provenance by status, and the count of named things it is out of.

    **`names_total` is every named thing, not the sum of the four columns**, which is the one place
    this series deviates from `milestones()` and `decisions()` above, and the deviation is the
    honest part. `ratified`, `recorded`, `provisional` and `unrecorded` are what a `Name:` block
    SAYS. A crate with no block at all has said nothing, and counting that silence as `unrecorded`
    would put a claim in the record that nobody made, which is the failure `script/names` refuses
    one directory over and the same reason `decisions()` will not read a statusless decision as
    DECIDED.

    So before milestone 115 invented the convention on 2026-08-04, the four columns are zero and
    `names_total` is the whole vocabulary of the tree, and the gap between them is the size of what
    was not yet being recorded. `script/lint` runs `script/names --check`, so in any tree from
    2026W32 onward the gap is zero and the sum is the total.

    A block that fails `script/names`' own form checks (a `ratified` with no date, a `recorded` with
    no citation) counts toward the total and toward no status, for the same reason: it has not said
    which of the four it is.
    """
    counts = dict.fromkeys(NAME_STATUSES, 0)
    surfaces = name_surfaces(tree)
    for _name, path, prefix in surfaces:
        text = tree.text.get(path)
        if text is None:
            continue
        found = name_provenance.block(text, prefix)
        if found is None:
            continue
        status, _date, _refused, problem = name_provenance.classify(found)
        if problem is None:
            counts[status] += 1
    return counts, len(surfaces)


def proposals(tree):
    """Unnumbered roadmap proposals: identified work filed where a script can see it, awaiting a number.

    **Nothing else on this dashboard can count these**, which is why the column exists.
    `milestones()` above matches index rows in `design/roadmap/README.md` and keys on a milestone
    NUMBER, and a proposal is defined by not having one, so it is invisible to that count by
    construction rather than by oversight.

    Counted the way `script/roadmap` counts them, out of the shared parse, so the two cannot
    disagree about what is in the pile. A file that does not parse as a proposal is not counted;
    `script/lint` runs `script/roadmap --check`, so in any tree since milestone 247 there are none.

    A promoted proposal is deleted rather than marked (calef, 2026-09-18), so it leaves this count
    by leaving the directory and no exclusion is needed here.

    The weeks before 2026-09-04 are zero because the directory did not exist, which is the same
    restatement artifact as the naming columns above and is rendered rather than backfilled.
    """
    count = 0
    for path, text in tree.markdown():
        directory, _slash, filename = path.rpartition('/')
        if directory != roadmap_proposals.DIRECTORY:
            continue
        if roadmap_proposals.filename_problem(filename):
            continue
        _when, _title, problem = roadmap_proposals.classify(text)
        if problem is None:
            count += 1
    return {'proposals_unnumbered': count}


def lines(tree):
    """Code and comment lines, `kernel/src` split from everything else.

    The split is the point rather than a convenience. AGENTS.md's second principle records that
    `kernel/src` measures about 40% comments deliberately, and a single tree-wide ratio hides
    exactly the thing that makes the number interesting.
    """
    out = {'kernel_code_lines': 0, 'kernel_comment_lines': 0,
           'other_code_lines': 0, 'other_comment_lines': 0}
    for path, text in tree.rust():
        total = non_blank(text)
        code = non_blank(strip_non_code(text))
        where = 'kernel' if path.startswith('kernel/src/') else 'other'
        out[where + '_code_lines'] += code
        out[where + '_comment_lines'] += total - code
    return out


# A `BUGS` section is a markdown heading in a note, a decision or a milestone block, or the same
# heading inside a Rust doc comment beside the feature it limits. Rising is GOOD: AGENTS.md calls
# these "not modesty, they are the mechanism", and a tree that grows features without growing
# honest limitations beside them is hiding them, not avoiding them.
_MD_BUGS = re.compile(r'^#{1,6} BUGS\b', re.M)
_RS_BUGS = re.compile(r'^[ \t]*(?:///|//!)[ \t]*#{1,6} BUGS\b', re.M)


def bugs(tree):
    return {'bugs_markdown': sum(len(_MD_BUGS.findall(t)) for _p, t in tree.markdown()),
            'bugs_rust': sum(len(_RS_BUGS.findall(t)) for _p, t in tree.rust())}


# The unsafe census (milestone 134) and the harness count, both from `scripts/rust_source.py`.
# The columns keep their `unsafe_` prefix here because a CSV header has no other namespace; the
# shared function's keys are the unprefixed ones a caller in `script/lint` reads.


def unsafe_census(tree):
    counts = rust_source.unsafe_census(tree.rust())
    return {'unsafe_' + key: value for key, value in counts.items()}


def harnesses(tree):
    counts = rust_source.harness_count(tree.rust())
    return {'harnesses_' + key: value for key, value in counts.items()}


def fatal_risks(tree):
    """How many of the nine claims that would kill nife have been put to an experiment.

    Tested versus untested only. A verdict column was considered and refused: three of the four
    statuses written so far say GREEN or AMBER in capitals, and risk 7's says neither ("RUN,
    2026-08-31, and it found the thing this risk exists to find"), so a colour series would be
    reading a sentence and guessing. The count of risks that have been RUN at all is the fact the
    file actually carries.
    """
    text = tree.text.get('design/fatal-risks.md')
    if not text:
        return {'fatal_risks_tested': 0, 'fatal_risks_untested': 0}
    parts = re.split(r'^## (\d+)\.', text, flags=re.M)
    tested = untested = 0
    for i in range(1, len(parts), 2):
        if re.search(r'^\*\*Status:', parts[i + 1], re.M):
            tested += 1
        else:
            untested += 1
    return {'fatal_risks_tested': tested, 'fatal_risks_untested': untested}


def coverage_from_lcov(path):
    """Aggregate line coverage from an lcov file, as a percentage to one decimal.

    LF and LH are lines-found and lines-hit per file, which is the same pair `script/coverage`'s
    per-file floor reads. This is the aggregate rather than the floor: the floor's job is to make
    one bad file loud, and a trend wants the whole.
    """
    found = hit = 0
    for line in pathlib.Path(path).read_text(errors='replace').split('\n'):
        if line.startswith('LF:'):
            found += int(line[3:])
        elif line.startswith('LH:'):
            hit += int(line[3:])
    if not found:
        raise SystemExit('metrics: %s records no lines; is it an lcov file?' % path)
    return '%.1f' % (100.0 * hit / found)


def row_for(rev, week, coverage=''):
    tree = Tree(rev)
    when = git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', rev).decode().strip()
    row = {'week': week, 'commit': rev[:12], 'date': when[:10],
           'coverage_lines_pct': coverage}
    ms = milestones(tree)
    for status in MILESTONE_STATUSES:
        row[_col('milestones', status)] = ms[status]
    row['milestones_total'] = sum(ms.values())
    dec = decisions(tree)
    for status in DECISION_STATUSES:
        row[_col('decisions', status)] = dec[status]
    row['decisions_total'] = sum(dec.values())
    nm, named = names(tree)
    for status in NAME_STATUSES:
        row[_col('names', status)] = nm[status]
    row['names_total'] = named
    for part in (proposals(tree), lines(tree), bugs(tree), harnesses(tree),
                 unsafe_census(tree), fatal_risks(tree)):
        row.update(part)
    return row


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Weeks, and which commit stands for one.

def week_of(iso_datetime):
    when = datetime.fromisoformat(iso_datetime).astimezone(timezone.utc)
    year, week, _day = when.isocalendar()
    return '%04dW%02d' % (year, week)


def week_commits():
    """{ISO week: the first-parent commit that stands for it}, oldest first.

    The greatest committer date inside the week, on the first-parent trunk. First-parent because
    that is the tree a reader means by "on that date"; committer date because a merge's author date
    can be days old, which would file a merge under the week its branch was cut.
    """
    out = {}
    log = git('log', '--first-parent', '--format=%H %cI', 'HEAD').decode().split('\n')
    for entry in log:
        if not entry.strip():
            continue
        sha, when = entry.split()
        out.setdefault(week_of(when), sha)   # log is newest-first, so the first seen is the latest
    return dict(sorted(out.items()))


def read_csv():
    if not CSV_PATH.exists():
        return {}
    text = CSV_PATH.read_text().strip().split('\n')
    header = text[0].split(',')
    rows = {}
    for line in text[1:]:
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        values = line.split(',')
        row = dict(zip(header, values))
        rows[row['week']] = row
    return rows


def write_csv(rows):
    """Rewrite the whole file from the dict, sorted, fixed columns, fixed formatting.

    Never appended to. This is the whole of the idempotence guarantee: the week being written
    replaces its own entry rather than adding one, so running twice in a week produces the same
    bytes, and so does running the backfill against a history that has not moved.
    """
    lines_out = [','.join(FIELDS)]
    for week in sorted(rows):
        row = rows[week]
        lines_out.append(','.join(str(row.get(field, '')) for field in FIELDS))
    DATA_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return '\n'.join(lines_out) + '\n'


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The charts.
#
# GitHub will not render an interactive chart, and it will not run a script in a markdown page, so
# a committed SVG is the only thing a reader actually sees. Written by hand rather than by a
# plotting crate: DECISIONS §46 (thin primitives or whole subsystems, and we write everything in
# between) makes taking one a decision, and a stacked bar is arithmetic and
# string formatting. The side benefit is that a diff can review one, which a binary PNG refuses.
#
# Colours are one categorical order with a LIGHT and a DARK step per hue, chosen for each surface
# rather than one set of hexes asked to serve both. Validated on 2026-09-19 with the dataviz
# skill's six-check validator against GitHub's own surfaces (#ffffff, #0d1117): every check passes
# in both modes, worst adjacent colour-blind separation 9.1 light and 8.4 dark (OKLab x100, target
# 8). This replaced Okabe-Ito, whose reputation is colour-blind separation and not contrast: its
# yellow read at 1.32:1 on white, which made the milestones chart's "Removed" band nearly
# invisible, and its gray failed the chroma floor. Three light steps still sit under 3:1 on white,
# which is legal only with relief, and each chart carries three kinds: the legend, a <title> on
# every segment, and weekly.csv as the table view. Slots are assigned in this fixed order and never
# cycled. The eighth slot was added on 2026-09-19 for the milestones chart's proposals, appended
# rather than inserted so that no existing series changed colour, and all eight were validated
# again in both modes before it shipped. A ninth series is never a new hue; it folds into another.

PALETTE_LIGHT = ['#2a78d6', '#eb6834', '#1baf7a', '#eda100', '#e87ba4', '#008300', '#4a3aa7',
                 '#e34948']
PALETTE_DARK = ['#3987e5', '#d95926', '#199e70', '#c98500', '#d55181', '#008300', '#9085e9',
                '#e66767']

CHART_W, CHART_H = 860, 380
# The charts show the most recent CHART_WEEKS weeks; weekly.csv keeps every week, and a backfill
# still restates all of them. calef, 2026-09-19: ten weeks, so W29 leaves the charts when W39
# arrives. Old weeks are not deleted, only no longer drawn, because the CSV is the table view
# the charts' relief rule leans on and a table that forgets is not one.
CHART_WEEKS = 10
PAD_L, PAD_R, PAD_T, PAD_B = 64, 210, 58, 56
LEGEND_WRAP = 22   # characters per legend line before a label wraps to a second


def esc(text):
    return (text.replace('&', '&amp;').replace('<', '&lt;').replace('>', '&gt;'))


def _wrap(text, width):
    """Split a legend label on word boundaries into lines of at most `width` characters.

    The legend column clipped six labels on 2026-09-19 because the width a label needs depends on
    whichever font the viewer's machine substitutes, and the arithmetic had assumed a narrow one.
    Wrapping is robust to that; a guess at glyph widths is not.
    """
    lines, line = [], ''
    for word in text.split():
        if line and len(line) + 1 + len(word) > width:
            lines.append(line)
            line = word
        else:
            line = word if not line else line + ' ' + word
    return lines + ([line] if line else [])


def week_label(week):
    """The key is the label. `2026W36` on the axis, in the CSV, and in the prose.

    calef ratified one spelling on 2026-09-02, after the page carried three: `2026-W30` in the
    narrative, `202636` on an axis, `2026-W29` in the data. This function exists so that if a
    future spelling is wanted there is exactly one place to change it, and so the axis cannot
    drift from the key it is derived from.
    """
    return week


def svg_chart(title, subtitle, weeks, series, path, ceiling=None):
    """One stacked bar per week, one colour per series, a legend on the right.

    `series` is [(label, [value per week])]. A single-element series is an ordinary bar chart,
    which is what the density and coverage panels are. `ceiling` is a number, labelled
    `ceiling`, or a (number, word) pair when the dashed line is something else: coverage's is
    `script/coverage`'s per-file floor, and labelling a floor a ceiling says the opposite.
    """
    line_word = 'ceiling'
    if isinstance(ceiling, tuple):
        ceiling, line_word = ceiling
    totals = [sum(s[1][i] for s in series) for i in range(len(weeks))]
    top = max(totals + ([ceiling] if ceiling else []) + [1])
    # An axis a person reads: gridlines at 1, 2, 2.5 or 5 times a power of ten, and whichever of
    # four, five or six intervals wastes the least height. Four alone rounds 231 up to 400.
    axis_top, ticks = None, 4
    for count in (4, 5, 6):
        raw = top / count
        magnitude = 10.0 ** math.floor(math.log10(raw)) if raw > 0 else 1.0
        for factor in (1, 2, 2.5, 5, 10):
            if magnitude * factor >= raw:
                candidate = magnitude * factor * count
                break
        if axis_top is None or candidate < axis_top:
            axis_top, ticks = candidate, count
    plot_w = CHART_W - PAD_L - PAD_R
    plot_h = CHART_H - PAD_T - PAD_B
    slot = plot_w / max(len(weeks), 1)
    bar_w = min(slot * 0.66, 54)

    def y_of(value):
        return PAD_T + plot_h - plot_h * value / axis_top

    out = ['<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="%d" height="%d" '
           'viewBox="0 0 %d %d" role="img" aria-label="%s">'
           % (CHART_W, CHART_H, CHART_W, CHART_H, esc(title)),
           '<style>',
           '  .bg{fill:#ffffff} .fg{fill:#1f2328} .mut{fill:#59636e} .grid{stroke:#d1d9e0}',
           '  .seg{stroke:#ffffff;stroke-width:1}',
           # A halo in the surface colour behind each value label, so a total that lands on the
           # dashed ceiling (the unsafe chart's 77s against its 88) stays legible.
           '  .val{paint-order:stroke;stroke:#ffffff;stroke-width:3px;stroke-linejoin:round}',
           ''.join('  .s%d{fill:%s}' % (j, c) for j, c in enumerate(PALETTE_LIGHT)),
           '  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){',
           '    .bg{fill:#0d1117} .fg{fill:#e6edf3} .mut{fill:#9198a1} .grid{stroke:#30363d}',
           '    .seg{stroke:#0d1117} .val{stroke:#0d1117}'
           + ''.join(' .s%d{fill:%s}' % (j, c) for j, c in enumerate(PALETTE_DARK)) + '}',
           '  text{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Helvetica,Arial,'
           'sans-serif}',
           '</style>',
           '<rect class="bg" width="%d" height="%d"/>' % (CHART_W, CHART_H),
           '<text class="fg" x="%d" y="26" font-size="16" font-weight="600">%s</text>'
           % (PAD_L - 44, esc(title)),
           '<text class="mut" x="%d" y="44" font-size="11">%s</text>'
           % (PAD_L - 44, esc(subtitle))]

    for tick in range(ticks + 1):
        value = axis_top * tick / ticks
        y = y_of(value)
        out.append('<line class="grid" x1="%d" y1="%.1f" x2="%.1f" y2="%.1f" '
                   'stroke-width="1"/>' % (PAD_L, y, PAD_L + plot_w, y))
        label = '%g' % value if value == int(value) else '%.1f' % value
        out.append('<text class="mut" x="%d" y="%.1f" font-size="10" text-anchor="end">%s</text>'
                   % (PAD_L - 8, y + 3, label))

    # The ceiling is drawn BEFORE the bars and their value labels, so a total landing on it
    # sits on top of the line rather than under it. It used to be drawn last and struck
    # through the unsafe chart's 77s, three of them, sitting just under its 88.
    if ceiling:
        y = y_of(ceiling)
        out.append('<line x1="%d" y1="%.1f" x2="%.1f" y2="%.1f" stroke="#D55E00" '
                   'stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>'
                   % (PAD_L, y, PAD_L + plot_w, y))
        out.append('<text class="mut" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" font-size="10">%s %s</text>'
                   % (PAD_L + plot_w + 6, y + 3, line_word, _number(ceiling)))

    for i, week in enumerate(weeks):
        x = PAD_L + slot * i + (slot - bar_w) / 2
        base = 0.0
        top_j = max((j for j, (_l, v) in enumerate(series) if v[i]), default=None)
        for j, (label, values) in enumerate(series):
            value = values[i]
            if not value:
                continue
            height = plot_h * value / axis_top
            y = y_of(base + value)
            # Every segment is stroked in the surface colour, which leaves a 2px gap between
            # neighbours so a stack reads as parts even where two fills are close in lightness.
            # Only the top of the whole bar is rounded: rounding every segment would make a
            # stack look like beads.
            tip = '<title>%s, %s: %s</title>' % (esc(week_label(week)), esc(label), _number(value))
            r = min(4.0, height / 2, bar_w / 2) if j == top_j else 0.0
            if r:
                d = ('M%.1f,%.1f V%.1f Q%.1f,%.1f %.1f,%.1f H%.1f Q%.1f,%.1f %.1f,%.1f V%.1f Z'
                     % (x, y + height, y + r, x, y, x + r, y, x + bar_w - r,
                        x + bar_w, y, x + bar_w, y + r, y + height))
                out.append('<path class="seg s%d" d="%s">%s</path>' % (j, d, tip))
            else:
                out.append('<rect class="seg s%d" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" width="%.1f" height="%.1f">'
                           '%s</rect>' % (j, x, y, bar_w, height, tip))
            base += value
        out.append('<text class="mut" x="%.1f" y="%d" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">%s</text>'
                   % (x + bar_w / 2, PAD_T + plot_h + 18, esc(week_label(week))))
        if totals[i]:
            out.append('<text class="fg val" x="%.1f" y="%.1f" font-size="10" '
                       'text-anchor="middle">%s</text>'
                       % (x + bar_w / 2, y_of(totals[i]) - 5, _number(totals[i])))


    legend_x = CHART_W - PAD_R + 22
    y = PAD_T + 6
    for j, (label, _values) in enumerate(series):
        out.append('<rect class="s%d" x="%d" y="%d" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/>'
                   % (j, legend_x, y))
        lines = _wrap(label, LEGEND_WRAP)
        spans = ''.join('<tspan x="%d" dy="%s">%s</tspan>'
                        % (legend_x + 17, '0' if k == 0 else '13', esc(line))
                        for k, line in enumerate(lines))
        out.append('<text class="fg" y="%d" font-size="11">%s</text>' % (y + 10, spans))
        y += 19 + 13 * (len(lines) - 1)
    out.append('</svg>')
    path.write_text('\n'.join(out) + '\n')


def _number(value):
    value = float(value)
    return '%d' % value if value == int(value) else '%.1f' % value


def _cell(row, field):
    raw = row.get(field, '')
    return float(raw) if raw not in ('', None) else 0.0


# One band on this page is arithmetic over columns rather than a column of its own. The naming
# chart's "no provenance block" is `names_total` minus the four statuses, and it is deliberately not
# in the CSV: it is not a status a `Name:` block can carry, and a fifth column would put a fifth
# word in a four-word vocabulary that `script/names` gates on. Drawn rather than stored, because the
# three weeks before milestone 115 invented the convention would otherwise be three empty bars,
# which this page's own rule reads as "the record did not exist" and would leave the reader with no
# idea that the tree already had 119 named things in it.
DERIVED = {
    'names_no_block': lambda row: max(
        0.0, _cell(row, 'names_total') - sum(_cell(row, _col('names', s)) for s in NAME_STATUSES)),
}


def series_of(rows, weeks, columns, scale=1.0):
    out = []
    for label, field in columns:
        values = []
        for week in weeks:
            derive = DERIVED.get(field)
            raw = derive(rows[week]) if derive else _cell(rows[week], field)
            values.append(raw / scale)
        if any(values):
            out.append((label, values))
    return out


CHARTS = [
    ('milestones.svg', 'Milestones by status, and proposals waiting for a number',
     'design/roadmap/README.md; unnumbered proposals from design/roadmap/proposals/ stacked on top',
     [('Built', 'milestones_built'), ('Partial', 'milestones_partial'),
      ('In progress', 'milestones_in_progress'), ('Not started', 'milestones_not_started'),
      ('Optional', 'milestones_optional'), ('Recorded', 'milestones_recorded'),
      ('Removed', 'milestones_removed'),
      ('Proposal, no number yet', 'proposals_unnumbered')], 1.0, None),
    ('decisions.svg', 'Architecture decisions by status',
     'design/decisions/README.md; before milestone 114 split it, a decision carried no status',
     [('Decided', 'decisions_decided'), ('Amended', 'decisions_amended'),
      ('Superseded', 'decisions_superseded'), ('Proposed', 'decisions_proposed'),
      ('No status recorded', 'decisions_unrecorded')], 1.0, None),
    ('names.svg', 'Names by what the tree records about them',
     'crates, programs, script/ entry points and Cargo packages; provenance lives in the header',
     [('Ratified', 'names_ratified'), ('Recorded', 'names_recorded'),
      ('Provisional', 'names_provisional'), ('Unrecorded', 'names_unrecorded'),
      ('No provenance block', 'names_no_block')], 1.0, None),
    ('lines.svg', 'Rust in the tree, in thousands of lines',
     'kernel/src split out, because it is commented on purpose; vendor/ excluded',
     [('kernel/src code', 'kernel_code_lines'), ('kernel/src comments', 'kernel_comment_lines'),
      ('other code', 'other_code_lines'), ('other comments', 'other_comment_lines')],
     1000.0, None),
    ('bugs.svg', 'BUGS sections, where a reader meets the feature',
     'rising is good: an honest limitation beside the thing it limits',
     [('in markdown', 'bugs_markdown'), ('in Rust doc comments', 'bugs_rust')], 1.0, None),
    ('harnesses.svg', 'Kani proof harnesses, and what can falsify them',
     'fatal risk 2: a proof nobody can turn red is not evidence',
     [('falsification on record', 'harnesses_falsified'),
      ('unfalsified', 'harnesses_unfalsified')], 1.0, None),
    ('unsafe.svg', 'unsafe blocks per 10,000 lines outside kernel/src/arch/',
     'the direction is down; the ceiling is script/lint’s',
     [('blocks per 10,000 lines', 'unsafe_density')], 1.0, 88),
    ('fatal-risks.svg', 'The nine things that would kill nife',
     'design/fatal-risks.md; tested means an experiment has been run, not that it came back green',
     [('experiment run', 'fatal_risks_tested'), ('not yet tested', 'fatal_risks_untested')],
     1.0, None),
    ('coverage.svg', 'Host-crate line coverage, per cent',
     'each week by its own script/coverage; 2026W29 predates the script',
     [('line coverage', 'coverage_lines_pct')], 1.0, (80, 'floor')),
]


def draw_charts(rows):
    weeks = sorted(rows)[-CHART_WEEKS:]
    DATA_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    for name, title, subtitle, columns, scale, ceiling in CHARTS:
        series = series_of(rows, weeks, columns, scale)
        if not series:
            series = [(columns[0][0], [0.0] * len(weeks))]
        svg_chart(title, subtitle, weeks, series, DATA_DIR / name, ceiling)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def print_table(row):
    width = max(len(f) for f in FIELDS)
    for field in FIELDS:
        print('%-*s  %s' % (width, field, row.get(field, '')))


def main(argv):
    coverage = ''
    coverage_week = ''
    args = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] in ('--coverage-from', '--coverage-for'):
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                raise SystemExit('metrics: %s needs an argument' % argv[i])
            if argv[i] == '--coverage-from':
                coverage = coverage_from_lcov(argv[i + 1])
            else:
                coverage_week = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
            continue
        args.append(argv[i])
        i += 1

    if coverage_week:
        # One cell, and only that cell. The row is deliberately NOT recomputed: that would repoint
        # its commit at whatever history says today and restate every count, which is --update's
        # job and is not what a caller measuring one historical week asked for.
        if args:
            raise SystemExit('metrics: --coverage-for takes no mode; it writes one cell and redraws')
        if not coverage:
            raise SystemExit('metrics: --coverage-for needs --coverage-from <lcov.info>')
        rows = read_csv()
        if coverage_week not in rows:
            raise SystemExit('metrics: %s is not a week in %s; --coverage-for only fills a row '
                             'that already exists' % (coverage_week, CSV_PATH))
        rows[coverage_week]['coverage_lines_pct'] = coverage
        CSV_PATH.write_text(write_csv(rows))
        draw_charts(rows)
        print('metrics: %s coverage_lines_pct = %s, charts in %s/'
              % (coverage_week, coverage, DATA_DIR))
        return 0

    mode = args[0] if args else '--table'
    if mode not in ('--table', '--csv', '--backfill', '--update', '--check', '--charts'):
        raise SystemExit('metrics: unknown argument %s; see the comment at the top of this script'
                         % mode)

    if mode == '--charts':
        draw_charts(read_csv())
        print('metrics: charts written to %s/' % DATA_DIR)
        return 0

    head = git('rev-parse', 'HEAD').decode().strip()
    if mode in ('--table', '--csv'):
        row = row_for(head, week_of(git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', head).decode().strip()),
                      coverage)
        if mode == '--csv':
            print(','.join(FIELDS))
            print(','.join(str(row.get(f, '')) for f in FIELDS))
        else:
            print_table(row)
        return 0

    weeks = week_commits()
    rows = read_csv()
    # Coverage survives a rewrite, including a `--backfill` that drops every other cell. It cannot
    # be re-derived from history (it needs an instrumented build at each checkout, and nothing here
    # is allowed to build), so the cell a caller filled from an lcov file is the only copy of that
    # measurement there will ever be. Milestone 276 found this the way it is usually found: running
    # `--backfill` to add a column, and watching 2026W36's 94.4 disappear with it.
    kept_coverage = {week: row.get('coverage_lines_pct', '') for week, row in rows.items()}
    current = week_of(git('show', '-s', '--format=%cI', head).decode().strip())
    if mode == '--backfill':
        rows = {}
        targets = weeks.items()
    else:
        # The current week, AND every week history has that the CSV does not. This is what closed
        # the 2026W37 gap: the weekly workflow fires at 06:00 UTC on Monday and used to write only
        # the week HEAD falls in, so when a lane merged after midnight (nine did on 2026-09-14, the
        # last eleven minutes before the cron) HEAD was already in the new week and the week that
        # had just finished was never written by anything. A missed or raced run now heals on the
        # next one, and `--check` below fails on a gap rather than passing it every time.
        missing = [w for w in weeks if w not in rows and w != current]
        targets = [(w, weeks[w]) for w in missing] + [(current, weeks.get(current, head))]

    # A fresh lcov describes the tree HEAD is in and nothing else, so it lands on that week only.
    # It used to be `coverage or kept` for every target, which meant `--backfill --coverage-from`
    # stamped one measurement across every week in the series and erased the backfilled cells.
    for week, rev in targets:
        fresh = coverage if week == current else ''
        rows[week] = row_for(rev, week, fresh or kept_coverage.get(week, ''))

    text = write_csv(rows)
    if mode == '--check':
        current_text = CSV_PATH.read_text() if CSV_PATH.exists() else ''
        if current_text != text:
            print('metrics: %s is stale; run script/metrics --update' % CSV_PATH, file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print('metrics: %s is current' % CSV_PATH)
        return 0

    CSV_PATH.write_text(text)
    draw_charts(rows)
    print('metrics: %d weeks in %s, charts in %s/' % (len(rows), CSV_PATH, DATA_DIR))
    return 0


sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
PYEOF
